        <div id="power-outage" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Do Pellet Stoves Work in a Power Outage? No, Not Without Backup Power
          </h2>

          <p>
            This is the question that matters most to our readers, so here is the plain answer. A standard pellet stove does not heat during a power cut. It burns wood pellets, not electricity, but nearly every part that keeps the fire going is electric. When the grid drops, the stove shuts down within minutes, and a hopper full of pellets does nothing for you.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What Runs on Electricity</h3>

          <p>
            Four parts need power. The auger is a motor-driven screw that moves pellets from the hopper into the burn pot. The combustion blower pulls air through the fire and pushes exhaust out of the vent. The room blower moves hot air off the heat exchanger and into the room. The igniter is a heating element that lights the pellets at start-up. On top of that, the control board, thermostat and safety switches all run on 120-volt household power. Replacement igniters for common models are listed at roughly 250 to 300 watts, and that draw lasts only for the few minutes of start-up. Once running, the stove draws far less, but it never draws zero.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What Happens When the Power Cuts Out</h3>

          <p>
            The auger stops, so fuel stops. The combustion blower stops, which is the part to take seriously: with no fan pushing exhaust out, smoke from the pellets still smouldering in the burn pot can drift back into the room until the fire dies. This is why manufacturers commonly ask for some vertical rise in the vent, so the exhaust has a natural draft path when the fan is off, and it is one of several reasons a pellet stove belongs in a house with a working <a href="/best-carbon-monoxide-detector/">carbon monoxide detector</a>.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How to Keep One Running: Battery Backup, Power Station or Generator</h3>

          <p>
            There are three workable plans. Some makers sell a battery backup unit made for their stoves, which switches over automatically and gives the stove time to burn down safely or keep running for a few hours. A <a href="/best-portable-power-station-for-home-backup/">portable power station for home backup</a> with a pure sine wave inverter can run a pellet stove for many hours once it is lit, because the running draw is modest; check the stove's manual for its wattage and size the battery accordingly. A generator works too, run outdoors and well away from windows, with a transfer switch or an approved inlet. Whichever you choose, test it before the first storm, not during it.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Non-Electric Exception</h3>

          <p>
            Gravity-fed pellet stoves, like the Wiseway on our list, use no electricity at all. They are the only pellet stoves that keep heating in an outage without backup, but they give up the thermostat, auto-ignition and blower. If outage heat with no extra equipment is your main goal, compare them with the <a href="/best-indoor-heater-for-power-outage/">indoor heaters that work without electricity</a> before deciding.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="sizing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. What Size Pellet Stove Do You Need? Trust BTU and Hopper Size, Not the Square-Foot Claim
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every pellet stove listing gives a heated area, and every one of those numbers is a best case. The rating assumes a well-insulated, fairly open home in a moderate climate. A drafty older house in a cold region can need a stove rated for twice the space it actually has.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Why the Square-Foot Rating Is Optimistic</h3>

          <p>
            Heat does not travel well through walls and around corners. A pellet stove heats the room it stands in very well, the rooms that open onto it reasonably well, and closed-off bedrooms down a hallway poorly. Treat the rating as a ceiling. Start from the space you actually want warm, then factor in your insulation, ceiling height and how cold your winters get. Where a listing gives BTU, as the PelPro does, that is a more honest number to compare.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Hopper Size Decides How Often You Fill It</h3>

          <p>
            Pellets come in 40 lb bags. A 40 lb hopper takes one bag, which in steady cold weather usually means a refill about once a day. A 120 lb or 150 lb hopper can go several days. If you work long hours or want the stove to run unattended overnight and through the next day, hopper size matters more than any other feature.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Freestanding or Fireplace Insert</h3>

          <p>
            A freestanding stove can go almost anywhere you can run a vent and give it the required clearances. An insert goes into an existing fireplace and usually needs a liner run up the chimney. If you already have a fireplace that loses heat, an insert is the better use of the space. If not, buy freestanding.
          </p>

