        <div id="safety" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Pellet Stove Safety: Venting, Clearances, Permits and a CO Alarm
          </h2>

          <p>
            A pellet stove is a combustion appliance, and it is installed for the long term, so the safety work happens mostly before the first fire. Get these four things right and a pellet stove is one of the cleaner, more controllable ways to burn fuel at home.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Venting: Use Listed Pellet Vent Pipe and Follow the Manual</h3>

          <p>
            Pellet stoves vent through purpose-made pellet vent pipe, usually 3 or 4 inches across, not ordinary stovepipe or dryer duct. Because the combustion fan pushes exhaust out, many can vent horizontally through a wall rather than needing a full chimney, which is a big part of their appeal. Even so, manuals commonly call for some vertical rise before the vent turns outside, so the exhaust can draft naturally if the power fails. Keep the termination away from windows, doors, decks and walkways, at the distances the manual sets.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Clearances and Floor Protection</h3>

          <p>
            Every stove has a label listing its minimum clearances to combustible walls and furniture, and the floor protection it needs underneath and in front. Those numbers come from the stove's safety listing, so treat them as the law for your install. Look for a stove listed to the relevant safety standard by a recognised lab, and check that it is EPA certified, which current US rules require for new pellet heaters.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Permits, Inspection and Your Insurer</h3>

          <p>
            Most areas require a permit and an inspection for a new solid-fuel appliance, and many home insurers want to know about it. A professional installation is the simplest way to get all three right. Skipping the permit can cause trouble with a claim later and with a home sale.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">A Carbon Monoxide Alarm Is Not Optional</h3>

          <p>
            Pellet stoves are sealed and vented, but a leaking vent joint, a blocked termination or smoke spill in a power cut can all put carbon monoxide into the room. Put a CO alarm on every sleeping level and near the stove's room. Our guide to the best <a href="/best-carbon-monoxide-detector/">carbon monoxide detector</a>s covers which ones to buy.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="pellet-vs-wood" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Pellet vs Wood Stove: Pellets Win on Convenience, Wood Wins in an Outage
          </h2>

          <p>
            Pellet and wood stoves both burn wood, but they live very different lives. Which one suits you comes down to how much tending you want to do and how much you care about heat during a power cut.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Where Pellet Stoves Win</h3>

          <p>
            A pellet stove behaves like a heating appliance. It lights itself, holds a set temperature with a thermostat, and runs for a day or more from one hopper fill. Pellets are dry and uniform, so the fire burns cleanly and predictably, and a 40 lb bag is easy to carry and stack. Venting through a wall is usually simpler and cheaper than building a full chimney.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Where Wood Stoves Win</h3>

          <p>
            A wood stove needs no electricity at all. In an outage it keeps heating, and many let you boil water or cook on top. If you have free or cheap firewood, the fuel can cost far less than bagged pellets. The price is labour: splitting, stacking, carrying and loading, plus a proper chimney. If outage resilience is your top priority, see our guide to the <a href="/best-wood-stove/">best wood stoves</a>.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How to Decide</h3>

          <p>
            If you want steady, hands-off heat and can add backup power, buy pellet. If you want heat that cannot be switched off by the grid and do not mind the work, buy wood. Some households do both.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="pellets" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. How Many Bags of Pellets Will You Burn? Plan on Roughly One a Day in Cold Weather
          </h2>

          <p>
            Fuel use depends on the stove, the setting and the weather, so any figure is an estimate. As a rough planning number, owners commonly report going through about one 40 lb bag a day when a stove runs steadily in cold weather, less on mild days or low settings, and more in a hard freeze with the stove on high.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Buy Certified Premium Pellets</h3>

          <p>
            Pellet quality shows up in the ash pan. Lower-grade pellets tend to leave more ash and clinkers in the burn pot, which means more cleaning and can mean an uneven burn. Look for bags marked with a recognised grading program, buy one bag of a new brand to try before buying a pallet, and stick with what burns cleanly in your stove.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Store Them Dry</h3>

          <p>
            Pellets absorb moisture and swell back into sawdust when they get wet, which can jam an auger. Keep bags off the floor and out of the weather, in a garage, shed or dry basement, and do not stack them against the stove.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Buy Before the Season</h3>

          <p>
            Pellets often sell out locally in a cold snap. Buying by the pallet before winter usually costs less per bag and means you are not hunting for fuel in the middle of a storm.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cleaning" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. How to Clean a Pellet Stove: A Little Daily, More Each Week, a Full Service Each Year
          </h2>

          <p>
            Pellet stoves are low-effort compared with wood, but not no-effort. Most problems owners report, such as a stove that will not light, a lazy flame or a sooty window, come down to skipped cleaning.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Daily: Clear the Burn Pot</h3>

          <p>
            With the stove cold or shut down as the manual directs, scrape ash and clinkers out of the burn pot so the air holes stay open. Blocked holes starve the fire of air, which is the most common cause of poor burning.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Weekly: Ash Pan, Glass and Heat Exchanger</h3>

          <p>
            Empty the ash pan into a metal container with a lid and keep it outdoors, away from the house, because ash can hold live embers for days. Wipe the glass with a cleaner made for stove glass, and use the heat exchanger rods or scraper if your stove has them.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Yearly: Vent and Full Service</h3>

          <p>
            Once a season, or by the tonnage the manual gives, have the vent pipe cleaned and the stove serviced: gaskets, blowers, igniter and the hidden ash passages. A clean vent matters for safety as much as for performance.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="worth-it" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Is a Pellet Stove Worth It? Yes, If You Will Keep It Powered and Fed
          </h2>

          <p>
            A pellet stove is a real heating system, priced like one once you add the vent kit, floor protection and installation. It pays off best when it carries a large share of your heating every winter, not when it sits as an occasional extra.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">When a Pellet Stove Pays Off</h3>

          <p>
            It makes the most sense where your main heat is expensive, such as electric resistance, propane or heating oil, and where you will run the stove most of the winter. It also suits people who want the look and warmth of a real fire without the labour of firewood.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">When Something Else Makes More Sense</h3>

          <p>
            If you mainly want the look of a fire, an <a href="/best-electric-fireplace/">electric fireplace</a> gives you that for a fraction of the cost and plugs into the wall. If you rent, cannot cut a vent, or only need occasional heat in one room, a portable heater is the sensible choice. And if your real worry is staying warm in a long outage without backup power, a wood stove or a non-electric pellet stove is the honest answer.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming a Pellet Stove Is Outage-Proof</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Buyers often pick a pellet stove for winter storms, then find it goes cold the moment the power fails. Unless it is a gravity-fed non-electric model, plan a battery backup, a power station with a pure sine wave inverter, or a generator before you need it, and test it.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying by the Square-Foot Claim</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The heated-area figure on a listing assumes a tight, open, well-insulated home. In an older house with closed rooms and a cold climate, the same stove heats much less. Size for the rooms you actually need warm, and compare BTU where the maker gives it.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the Permit and the Manual's Clearances</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A pellet stove is a permanent solid-fuel appliance. Installing it without a permit, at smaller clearances than the label allows, or with the wrong vent pipe can void the warranty, cause trouble with your insurer, and create a real fire risk.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Running It Without a CO Alarm</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Pellet stoves are sealed and vented, but a leaking vent joint or smoke spill in a power cut can still put carbon monoxide into the house. A working CO alarm on every sleeping level costs little and is the one safety device that warns you in time.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Letting the Burn Pot Clog</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Most lighting failures and lazy, sooty flames come from ash and clinkers blocking the burn pot's air holes. Scrape it daily in heavy use, empty the ash pan weekly into a lidded metal can kept outdoors, and have the vent cleaned every season.
              </p>
            </div>
