        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best pellet stove?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most homes, the ComfortBilt HP22 is the best pellet stove: it is rated to heat up to 2,800 square feet, takes a full 40 lb bag in its 55 lb hopper, and has a long track record for parts. Choose the PelPro PP150 for the longest time between fills, or the Castle Serenity for a smaller home.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do pellet stoves work without electricity?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Standard pellet stoves do not. The auger, combustion fan, room blower, igniter and controls all need household power, so the stove shuts down in an outage. To keep heating, you need a battery backup, a power station with a pure sine wave inverter, or a generator. Gravity-fed non-electric pellet stoves are the exception and run with no power at all.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long will a battery backup run a pellet stove?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It depends on the battery's capacity and the stove's running wattage, which is listed in its manual. Start-up draws far more than running because the igniter is on, so it is best to light the stove on grid power or a generator. Divide the battery's usable watt-hours by the stove's running watts for a rough estimate.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a pellet stove vent through a wall?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually, yes. Because a pellet stove uses a fan to push exhaust out, most can vent horizontally through an exterior wall using listed pellet vent pipe, without a full chimney. Manuals commonly call for some vertical rise first, so the stove drafts naturally if the power fails, and set distances from windows, doors and walkways.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How many bags of pellets does a pellet stove use per day?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Roughly one 40 lb bag a day is a common figure when a stove runs steadily in cold weather, though use varies widely with the stove, the setting and the weather. Mild days and low settings use much less, and a hard freeze with the stove on high uses more. Buy a pallet before winter to avoid shortages.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a pellet stove cheaper to run than electric heat?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It often is, compared with electric resistance heat, propane or heating oil, but it depends on local prices for pellets and energy. A pellet stove also has real upfront costs for the stove, vent kit, floor protection and installation, so it pays off best when it carries much of your heating every winter.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need a permit to install a pellet stove?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                In most areas, yes. A new solid-fuel appliance usually needs a permit and an inspection, and many insurers want to be told about it. Requirements vary by location, so check with your local building department first. A professional installer will normally handle the permit and make sure clearances, floor protection and venting meet the manual and local code.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
